# =============================================================================
# HELLO-NODE TUTORIAL APPLICATION - ENVIRONMENT CONFIGURATION TEMPLATE
# =============================================================================
#
# This file provides a template for environment variable configuration for the
# Node.js tutorial application featuring a single GET /hello endpoint, which
# returns the plain-text greeting "Hello world" to the calling HTTP client.
#
# HOST and PORT below are the COMPLETE configuration surface of this project.
# Both are optional, and both fall back to a default written down here rather
# than hidden in the code (see readConfig() in server.js).
#
# HOW TO USE THIS FILE:
# 1. Read it. Every variable this project understands is documented below with
#    its default, so the whole configuration surface is written down in one
#    place instead of being discovered by reading the code
# 2. Change nothing to follow the tutorial: 'npm start' with neither variable
#    set binds localhost:3002, which is what README.md documents
# 3. To use a different value, put it in the ENVIRONMENT of the command you
#    run - the single-run form is shown directly below
# 4. Do not copy this file to .env expecting it to take effect. Nothing in this
#    project parses a .env file, so a copy is inert: the values below would be
#    read by a human and ignored by the process. (A .env file you create for
#    your own notes is ignored by the repository root .gitignore, so 'git add'
#    will not pick it up by accident - keep it out of version control anyway,
#    because an ignore rule is a default rather than a prohibition: 'git add -f'
#    commits an ignored path, and a file already tracked stays tracked. This
#    template is tracked deliberately, so every learner starts from the same
#    documented values.)
#
# HOW THESE VARIABLES ACTUALLY REACH THE SERVER:
# server.js has no 'dotenv' dependency and none is being added, so a .env file
# is NOT read by the process. This template documents the two variables for a
# human; readConfig() reads process.env, so a real override has to be present in
# the environment - set it on the command line for a single run:
#
#   PORT=4010 HOST=127.0.0.1 npm start
#   -> Server listening on http://127.0.0.1:4010
#
# RUNNING THE TEST SUITE WITH THESE VARIABLES SET IS FINE:
# Setting or exporting HOST and PORT configures 'npm start' and has no effect on
# 'npm test'. The suite does not depend on a clean caller environment: the
# defaults test calls readConfig({}) with an explicit empty environment, and the
# test that covers the defaulted 'env = process.env' parameter sets its own
# sentinel values and restores whatever was there in a 'finally' block
# (test/unit/server.test.js).
#
# SECURITY NOTE: This template contains only example values, no actual secrets
# or credentials. The .env file should never be committed to version control.
#
# =============================================================================

# =============================================================================
# SERVER CONFIGURATION
# =============================================================================

# Server Port Configuration
# Description: TCP port the HTTP server binds, on which GET /hello is served
# Default: 3002 (free in this repository - see PORT ALLOCATION below)
# Validation: Integer between 1024-65535 (non-privileged ports)
# Usage: readConfig() in server.js, as Number(env.PORT) || DEFAULT_PORT
#
# PORT ALLOCATION IN THIS REPOSITORY:
# - 3000 is taken. The development compose service publishes it on the host as
#   "3000:3000" (infrastructure/docker/docker-compose.yml:86) and the image
#   exposes it (infrastructure/docker/Dockerfile:120), so binding it here would
#   fail for anyone running 'docker compose up'. It is also a whitelisted CORS
#   origin of the sibling Flask service (src/backend/app.py:271).
# - 3001 is taken by the production compose service (docker-compose.yml:231)
# - 5678 is taken by the Python debugger, debugpy (docker-compose.yml:87)
# - 8000 is taken by the sibling Flask service, which defaults to it in code
#   (src/backend/wsgi.py:106 and src/backend/app.py:722)
# - 3002 is claimed by nothing else in this repository, and stays adjacent
#   enough to the familiar 3000 to remain recognizable to a learner
#
# WHY PORT=0 DOES NOT REQUEST AN EPHEMERAL PORT:
# readConfig() falls back with '||', and Number('0') is falsy, so this reader
# treats a zero as absent: PORT=0 resolves to the default 3002 rather than
# asking the operating system for any free port. An ephemeral port is a testing
# affordance reached only by calling startServer({ port: 0 }) directly
# in-process; it is not a documented operator option.
#
# ON THE SIBLING PROJECT'S VALUE:
# The Python template in this repository sets PORT=3000
# (src/backend/.env.example:38). That is that project's value, not a conflict to
# resolve here - the two projects bind different ports on purpose so that both
# can run side by side on one machine.
#
# SECURITY: Staying in the non-privileged range avoids binding a port that
# requires elevated permissions, and a port no other service claims avoids one
# process silently shadowing another
PORT=3002

# Server Host Configuration
# Description: Network interface the HTTP server binds
# Default: localhost (the loopback interface only - reachable from this machine
#          and from nowhere else, the safe default for code run on a laptop)
# Validation: Valid hostname, IP address, or 0.0.0.0 for all interfaces
# Usage: readConfig() in server.js, as env.HOST || DEFAULT_HOST, then handed
#        straight to server.listen(port, host)
#
# DEPLOYMENT NOTES:
# - Local Development: Use 'localhost' to keep a tutorial server off every
#   external interface
# - Container Deployment: Use '0.0.0.0' for accessibility across container
#   networks, the same guidance the sibling template gives
#   (src/backend/.env.example:46-49); the Python module defaults to it for
#   exactly that reason (src/backend/wsgi.py:105)
#
# THIS VALUE IS NOT VALIDATED:
# It is passed through to listen() as given, so setting 0.0.0.0 really does bind
# every interface on the machine. Rejecting a value the sibling runtime treats
# as legitimate would be a surprising restriction in a tutorial: the default is
# the safe one, and the override is the operator's decision.
#
# WHAT THE STARTUP BANNER PRINTS:
# server.js reads the bound PORT back from server.address(), but it prints this
# HOST value verbatim - so the banner echoes whatever you set here. With the
# default it prints http://localhost:3002, an address a client can call as
# shown. With HOST=0.0.0.0 it prints http://0.0.0.0:3002, and that is a wildcard
# bind address rather than a destination: call such a server on a concrete
# address of one of the interfaces it is now listening on, for example
# http://127.0.0.1:3002 from the same machine.
#
# SECURITY: Host binding controls which network interfaces can reach the server
HOST=localhost

# =============================================================================
# END OF TEMPLATE
# =============================================================================
# The two variables above are every environment variable this project reads.
# server.js deliberately reads no NODE_ENV, no LOG_LEVEL and no secret of any
# kind: a template listing a variable the code ignores would teach a learner
# something false.
# =============================================================================
